Dear Mr. Wilcox,

I hope this letter finds you in good health and high spirits. I am writing
to express my agreement to the renewal of the tenancy agreement for my
apartment at 10-585 Corydon Ave. I would also like to extend my
congratulations to the entire team at Thorwin Properties for their recent
victory in the rent increase dispute. A 32.30% rent increase for an
unrenovated suite during a rent freeze year is a very impressive
accomplishment!

While I appreciate the efforts made by the Deputy Commissioner of the
Residential Tenancies Commission to reach a fair decision, I must admit
that I am disappointed with the outcome. It is disheartening to learn that,
=E2=80=98most of the evidence and submissions provided were deemed irreleva=
nt to
the crucial questions necessary to decide on the appeal.=E2=80=99 Photograp=
hic
evidence, a neat table of the landlord=E2=80=99s history of rent increase a=
bove
guidelines, and commentary on the socioeconomic reality facing Manitobans
were simply not enough to persuade our government=E2=80=99s agents.

Nonetheless, I would like to take this opportunity to acknowledge the
skills and determination of Thorwin Properties in achieving their desired
rent increase. It usually takes exceptional acumen and persuasive arguments
to succeed in such matters, and your accomplishment is certainly
commendable especially when considering that none of the arguments I made
were directly addressed and responded to during our hearing.

The rent freeze was enacted with the intention of safeguarding tenants from
exorbitant increases, and it is regrettable that circumvention of this
legislation has occurred through capital expenditures. This practice, in my
view, raises ethical concerns as it undermines the very legislation
designed to protect tenants like myself. It highlights a pervasive issue in
the socio-economic state of our world, where corporate interests often
prevail over the rights and well-being of individuals.

The table from my appeal displays historic rent increases, which further
underscores the widening disparity between tenants' financial capacity and
the escalating costs of living. Despite being approved by a government
agency, the undeniable reality of this injustice cannot be ignored and yet
it has.

I would like to emphasize that my decision to agree to the renewal of the
tenancy agreement does not signify my acceptance of the aforementioned
practices. As a tenant, I have always strived to be an excellent resident,
respectful of the property and considerate of my neighbors. I will continue
to uphold these standards during the forthcoming tenancy period, just as I
have since becoming a resident prior to Thorwin Properties' ownership.

In conclusion, congratulations once again on your victory. I hope that this
letter conveys my genuine desire to maintain a cordial and professional
relationship moving forward, despite my concerns regarding the recent
decision. I trust that the upcoming tenancy period will be mutually
beneficial and that future resolutions will reflect a more equitable and
just approach to tenant-landlord relations.

Thank you for your attention to this matter. Please feel free to contact me
if there are any further details or documentation required.

Yours sincerely,

Evan Rosner
